  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSome showers have developed over the southeastern portion of the\nforecast area early this morning with a piece of mid-level\nenergy moving through the Upper Ohio River Valley, tapping into\na moist environment. These showers should exit over the next\ncouple of hours. Immediately behind these showers, conditions\nare clearing and allowing for a mix of fog and low stratus to\nform across the area. This should only continue to build\novernight and become more locally dense with time. There will\nlikely need to be at least a Special Weather Statement for the\nvisibility at some point this morning, but a Dense Fog Advisory\ncannot be ruled out.\n\nAfter some recovery during the late morning hours, the main\nconcern of the day will be additional showers and storms\ndeveloping ahead of a cold front. By early afternoon,\ntemperatures should be in the 80s with dew points in the upper\n60s/lower 70s. This will present enough of a warm, moist air\nmass to support storm development with modest MLCAPE values.\nWith the storms and cold front, there will be support with the\nright entrance region of an upper level jet and 0-6 km bulk\nshear values will be around 40 kts, which will allow for some\norganization of storm activity. The main concerns with storms\nwould be gusty to damaging wind gusts, although some hail could\nbe possible with the strongest storms. The limiting factor for\nstorms within the forecast area will be real estate. The front\nwill be moving from northwest to southeast and convective\ninitiation will be over the local area. By the time storms\nbecome strong enough to merit any wind or hail hazards, they may\nbe south of I-90 and east of I-71 with very little of the\nforecast area left to work with.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe middle of the week forecast continues to be unsettled as a\npseudo-cold front will approach the region from the northwest\nas the upper ridge over/south of the area will break down a bit.\nThis front will generate a round of showers and storms on\nTuesday night, as some mid- and upper-level energy will be\nallowed into the lower Great Lakes region. The frontal boundary\nwill stall out over the area and the local area will be between\na trough to the north and a ridge to the south. With warm and\nhumid conditions over the region supplied by the ridge to the\nsouth, expect scattered shower and storm chances through at\nleast Thursday with an unsettled pattern in place. One note of\nearly concern is that there will be the west-east oriented\nsurface boundary over the region with zonal west-east flow aloft\nthat could favor repeated rounds of storms over the same areas,\nwhich could pose heavy rain and flooding concerns, depending on\nhow things end up later this week.",
